**The check has its own database.** It runs in a database of its own on the scratch server, made for that run and dropped afterwards, and the database `_morm_proof` uses never holds the extension, so R-1067 keeps its limits. A scratch user that isn't superuser couldn't take `EXECUTE` on the extension's functions from `_morm_proof`, so R-1067 would stop `makemigrations` if the extension were there. That user also gets `42501` from `CREATE EXTENSION plpgsql_check`, because the extension isn't trusted, so it can't add the extension to a database of its own either. So a superuser makes a template database called `morm_plpgsql_check`, once per scratch server, and installs the extension in that:

```sql
CREATE DATABASE morm_plpgsql_check;
\c morm_plpgsql_check
CREATE EXTENSION plpgsql_check;
\c postgres
ALTER DATABASE morm_plpgsql_check IS_TEMPLATE true ALLOW_CONNECTIONS false;
```

morm makes each check database with `CREATE DATABASE ... TEMPLATE morm_plpgsql_check`, which needs only the `CREATEDB` that `makemigrations` already needs, replays the chain and `up` into it, runs the check as the scratch user, and drops it, after a stop too. A scratch user that's a superuser and finds no template makes it the same way. The extension doesn't go in `template1`, because the database `_morm_proof` uses would get it too.

